// Contour of the Catalan Countries, computed and stored LOCALLY inside this file
// (nothing is downloaded at runtime). Source: OpenStreetMap, via the per-province/
// department extracts from "hyperknot/country-levels-export" (Barcelona, Girona,
// Lleida, Tarragona, València, Alacant, Castelló, Illes Balears, Pyrénées-Orientales,
// and Andorra), merged with Turf.js. Far more coastline resolution than the official
// GISCO/Eurostat source used before (up to 19,000 points at the max level, vs 2,800).
//
// La Franja de Ponent's boundary is built from two cross-checked sources: the
// IEC/ésAdir list of 61 Catalan-speaking municipalities (esadir.cat/toponims/
// paisoscatalans/municipisfranja) and the reference map embedded in
// ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franja_de_Ponent (Mapa_de_la_Franja_de_Ponent.svg),
// pixel-sampled after re-registering its hand-traced coordinates against real OSM
// geometry. The two sources mostly agree; where they don't, the map wins, since
// it also encodes the separate Benasquès category the ésAdir list doesn't cover:
// Isàvena and Tor-la-ribera are shown Catalan on the map despite being absent from
// ésAdir (each is a merged/boundary-crossing municipality where only part is
// Catalan-speaking — same situation as Graus and Capella, which stay excluded);
// Vall de Lierp is shown included too, while its neighbour Vall de Bardají and
// Foradada del Toscar and Campo are shown outside. Excluded throughout: Graus,
// Capella (Aragonese-speaking town centres — only specific outlying hamlets
// within them are Catalan-speaking, not the whole municipality), and the
// Perarrúa/Secastilla/Santaliestra/La Fueva/Abizanda block the map marks in a
// third color ("historically Catalan per the GEC encyclopedia, but not part of
// the current Franja"). The Benasquès-speaking municipalities (Benasc, Bisaurri,
// Castejón de Sos, Chía, Sesué, Sahún, Vilanova d'Éssera) are included per the
// map's own legend, since ésAdir's list is Catalan-only and silent on Benasquès.
// El Baix Cinca uses its official comarca (five municipalities, all Catalan-
// speaking); el Matarranya uses its 29-municipality natural/historic boundary
// (matches ésAdir exactly: ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matarranya_(comarca_històrica)
// plus Faió, Nonasp, Favara and Maella, the Catalan-speaking Baix Aragó-Casp
// villages). Every municipality was fetched individually from OSM (not the
// official comarca outlines, which also cover plain-Aragonese-speaking
// territory) and unioned with Turf.js; the result merges into a single connected
// landmass with mainland Catalonia with no gaps or unrenderable holes —
// Ribagorça/Llitera and Baix Cinca/Matarranya only touch each other via the
// mainland, since a non-Catalan wedge (Esplús/Cinca Mitjà) separates them
// directly.
//
// El Carxe (OSM relation 11755232, tagged place=region / political_division=
// linguistic_community — not an administrative boundary, since it's only part of
// the Murcian municipalities of Iecla, Jumella and Favanella, per
// ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Carxe) is unioned into the mainland rather than kept as
// a separate ring: it directly borders (touches, not just is near) the Valencian
// municipalities of el Pinós and l'Alguenya, so it's a peninsula of the mainland,
// not a true exclave. Re-simplified per tier along with the rest of the mainland
// ring, unlike l'Alguer below.
//
// L'Alguer (OSM relation 40581, its main municipal polygon only — 7 tiny offshore
// islet fragments in the raw multipolygon were dropped) IS a genuine exclave —
// nowhere near the mainland — so it's kept as one more disjoint ring, reused
// unchanged at every zoom tier rather than re-simplified per tier like the rest of
// the coastline.

